From 1f337ffa18f1cf8417b6521169a3557f790edb03 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Josep Torra <n770galaxy@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 16:19:54 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 50/57] drm/gma500: run sgxfive render jobs from userspace
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Implement render contexts and render jobs (SGXhumation's
design/sgx545_render_uapi.md §11).

A render context keeps its firmware structures in its kernel memory and
gets two slots in its VM's reserved render heaps for what the TA, the DPM
and the 3D pipe write: the parameter buffer with its host-built page table,
the scratch primitive block, the background object and the RT data's
buffers. The microkernel loads a parameter buffer through the context's
page directory, so a VM with render contexts aliases the firmware buffers
and its contexts' kernel memory. The aliases are read-only except the TA/3D
control block, which the microkernel writes in that window: read-only, the
write faults and the render stalls (seen on the laptop; debugfs
alias_ta3d_ro reproduces it). This lets a render client's shaders write the
microkernel's TA/3D control block, one more reason the render node is for
trusted users only.

A render job is built at submission into its hardware fence, as a TA
command and render details from the shared builders, with the 3D status
word as the fence. One render job per context is in the firmware at a time
(prepare_job waits for the previous one), so running it may rewrite the
render details and the background object; it counts the scene in the RT
data set's pending count and kicks the TA queue. Commands are checked: the
control stream and the pixel-event program in user heaps and aligned, the
render target at most 256x128 (more than eight tile rows faults on page 0
of the parameter slot), ISP_BGOBJ bits 8 and 9 only.

Recovery rebuilds every render context and writes its unfinished job back
from the fence's copy. Destroying a context sends CLEANUP for its RT data
set, render context and parameter buffer and waits for HostCtl's
acknowledgement (0.5-2 ms on the laptop; the RT data set is only cleaned
up once its pending count equals the completed one), with a blame-free
reset as the fallback. Error states show render contexts and their TA
status, and save the whole TA command.

On the laptop the first triangle renders from userspace exactly at sizes
from 32x16 to 256x128, with several contexts and VMs, interleaved with
transfers and syncobjs; a faulting render is recovered in about 100 ms.

